% Graph editor (the Examples page)
%
% The Logical English (for LPS) twin of an Epilog program with operations, written by the Epilog translator
% from grapheditor.rules (kept in sources/): the operations are causal laws, the
% views over the state intensional fluents.
% The scenario applies chosen operations to the data sheet (kept in sources/), as EpilogJS
% applied them; LPS2's final state is checked against EpilogJS's (test_epilog.pl).
% Source: the Epilog Examples page, http://epilog.stanford.edu/homepage/rulesheet.php?theory=grapheditor
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program was written by a translator from a program of another
% system. It is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or
% implied, including any warranty that it is accurate, complete or fit for a
% particular purpose. The translation may be wrong: check it against its source
% before relying on it. It is not legal, tax, insurance, financial or other
% professional advice. Its authors accept no liability for any loss or damage
% arising from its use.

the target language is: lps.

the maximum time is 5.

the actions are:
    the move copy with *a thing* and *a second thing* is made; known as copy.
    the move insert with *a thing* and *a second thing* is made; known as insert.
    the move invert with *a thing* is made; known as invert.

the fluents are:
    *a thing* is the edge of *a second thing*; known as edge.

the knowledge base grapheditor includes:

the text of the game is at "sources/grapheditor.rules".

when the move copy with a thing and a second thing is made
    and the thing is the edge of a third thing
then the second thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
then the thing is the edge of the second thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
    and the second thing is the edge of a third thing
then the thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
    and a fourth thing is the edge of a third thing
    and the second thing is the edge of the fourth thing
then the thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
    and a fourth thing is the edge of a third thing
    and a fifth thing is the edge of the fourth thing
    and the second thing is the edge of the fifth thing
then the thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
    and a fourth thing is the edge of a third thing
    and a fifth thing is the edge of the fourth thing
    and a sixth thing is the edge of the fifth thing
    and the second thing is the edge of the sixth thing
then the thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move insert with a thing and a second thing is made
    and a fourth thing is the edge of a third thing
    and a fifth thing is the edge of the fourth thing
    and a sixth thing is the edge of the fifth thing
    and a seventh thing is the edge of the sixth thing
    and the second thing is the edge of the seventh thing
then the thing is the edge of the third thing.

when the move invert with a thing is made
    and the thing is the edge of a second thing
then the second thing is the edge of the thing
    and it is not the case that the thing is the edge of the second thing.

% RESIDUE deep_operations_1 BEGIN: the move insert/2 calls operations more than five levels deep
% TODO: translate the fragment below by hand, or with the Contract Assistant (residue mode); it was not translated automatically
% operations are inlined into the move's causal laws to a depth of five calls: the effects reached through deeper calls (or a recursive operation) have no law; state them as laws by hand
%   epilog:
%   | insert(X,Y) :: edge(X,Y)
%   | insert(X,Y) :: edge(Y,Z) ==> insert(X,Z)
% RESIDUE deep_operations_1 END

initially "a" is the edge of b
    and b is the edge of c
    and c is the edge of d.

% EpilogJS's state after the play (the twin's final state under LPS2 must be the same):
%   - edge(a,b)
%   - edge(b,c)
%   - edge(d,c)
%   - edge(e,b)
%   - edge(x,b)
%   - edge(x,c)
scenario play is:
    the move copy with "a" and e is made from 1 to 2.
    the move invert with c is made from 2 to 3.
    the move insert with x and b is made from 3 to 4.

